today we're going to be covering blend  modes in webgl  and 3js understanding how and why they  work  and as an added bonus i'm going to show  you a much more advanced way to do  blending  so since we're covering blend modes we  won't be doing as much code as normal  now if you already know everything there  is to know about this topic  feel free to skip ahead to whatever time  it is i fill in here  if you just came here for the additive  alpha explanation  first off i've got a little simple  project setup going here  as you can see there's not much to it  mostly just the same basic setup we used  to create a 3d basic world tutorial  this is intentionally pretty simple over  here i've got some quads that i'm going  to render onto the screen  and these lines here change the blend  mode so that we can see the effects of  different blend modes  running it just shows a quad along with  some info on the side  the whole point of this thing is that  we'll be able to observe how changes to  the blend mode affect the overall image  here i'll go flip the blend mode so if i  change this line here to additive and  re-rerun this  you can see that the images change and  we've got this one that's blending over  top the  other using this additive blend mode  and we can swap this for other blend  modes and the result shows up in the  project  let's take a step back now and  understand what exactly is going on here  how webgl  and 3gs blend one image onto another how  that's controlled essentially  let's start with some terminology you've  got the destination  or as it's often shortened to dst  which is what's already on the screen in  essence  then you've got the source or src and  that's the image you're compositing onto  the destination  and all this is controlled by a couple  functions that you can use to change  how things are blended onto the screen  how the source  and the destination are blended together  the first up is the blend function now  in webgl this is called gl.blendfunc  in 3gs you would supply values for the  blend source and blend dust  parameters on a material and these in  essence supply multipliers for the  source and destination colors  respectively  and there's a bunch of constants here  that can be supplied you can look over  the list here  let's step through a quick example let's  say that you supply the multipliers uh  one and zero so that means that for  every pixel on the screen  the final color is equal to the source  times one  plus the destination times zero which of  course  is just the source  if i change the values here on this line  and we run it you can see that the image  completely overwrites the background  let's try some other multipliers so  let's go with one  one so in this case that means the color  for every pixel on the screen  is a straight up addition of the old  color plus the new color  so the final color is equal to source  times one  plus destination times one let's go and  change this to 1  1 in the code and now that we run it you  can see that our image gets blended over  top  making the background brighter and one  thing to note here is that remember that  the edges of the image were black  and black is basically zeros so when you  add zero to the background color  nothing happens so this only brightens  up in the middle  now the second part of what controls  blending is the blend equation  and up until now our examples have  always added the first  and second parts together i.e it's  always been source  times source factor plus destination  times a destination factor  webgl and thus 3gs also define ways to  modify the equation itself  in webgl this is gl.blend equation and  in 3gs these are the predefined blending  equations  what this allows you to do is change how  source and destination are combined  so instead of addition if we want to  change this to subtraction  the previous example changes as well so  let's step through we'll use one one  again as our blending factors  but we use subtraction as our blending  equation  the final color for every pixel on the  screen would thus become source  times one minus destination times one  we can go into the code and change this  to subtraction and when we run it it  looks a lot different than the additive  version  notice the color changes as you go  towards the center where previously it  got lighter  it might seem that there's a like a  million options here  and there are but a lot of effects  mostly boil down to two modes  additive and alpha blending additive  blending is that glowy fiery look  that you get and we actually already  covered it it's the  source times one plus destination times  one example that we ran through earlier  and alpha blending isn't conceptually  that hard either  its source time source alpha plus  destination times  one minus source alpha which you may  notice is just a straight up linear  interpolation between the two colors  based on the alpha value  now if i use a texture with alpha and  change the code here to use alpha  blending  you get something that's composited over  top of the background  instead of brightening it up now let's  talk about why having a separate  additive and alpha kind of sucks  this is a neat trick that i actually  discovered when i was working on an xbox  360 slash  playstation 3 title back when i first  started working  first off let's look at the problem it's  solving let's say that i have a raging  fire going on  now fire also should have a bunch of  black smoke and ash coming out so let's  add some smoke coming out the top  if you have separate additive and alpha  blending modes then this has to be done  as two separate particle systems  meaning as we move the camera around one  of them has to draw in front of the  other either the smoke gets drawn first  or the fire does  or they might even pop from front to  back depending on the camera angle  either way it's not going to look great  contrast that too if i combine this all  into a single blend mode which means  that it's all a single particle system  look at what happens as i move the  camera around it's all seamless  way better looking to understand how  it's done we need to go back to the  blend factors for both additive and  alpha blending  so additive blending looks like this 1 1  or it can also be source alpha 1 where  you just want to have an option to  control the overall  fade with something other than just the  color and  alpha blending looks like source alpha  and one minus source alpha  so an additive the final color is equal  to source times source alpha  plus destination times one and in alpha  blending the final color is equal to  source times source alpha  plus destination times one minus source  alpha  and we need these to be the same  equation somehow  you can do it like this our new additive  alpha combination equation is going to  be source times 1  plus destination times 1 minus source  alpha  and we're going to have to write  ourselves a special shader  so this shader it needs to do two things  it needs to do the alpha blending itself  so here in the fragment shader we  multiply the final color by the alpha  by doing that the blend mode we  specified works exactly the same as  alpha blending  see source times 1 plus destination  times 1 minus source alpha since we're  manually multiplying the final color by  source alpha ourselves  this is exactly the same as alpha  blending  watch i'll change the blend mode here in  our example program and this works just  like alpha blending  next for this to do additive blending we  need the one minus source alpha part  to be equal to one that's easy  we need the source alpha to be zero so  that means that one minus zero is equal  to one  and we can do that by having a special  blend mode value in the shader that gets  passed down per particle  and we simply multiply the alpha value  by that  so if the blend value is one the shader  outputs a normal alpha value  and if the blend value is zero the alpha  value turns into zero  and this whole thing becomes additive  and that's it  once you do those two small tweaks you  have a combined blend mode that does  both  and your particle system takes a giant  leap forward in both simplicity  and in looks hope you enjoyed this  make sure to like and subscribe as  always leave a comment and especially  let me know if there's a subject you  want covered and i'll add it to my to-do  list  source code is up on github blah blah  blah do whatever you want with it just  don't ask me to support it  i'll see you next time cheers

